  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Crudan- DRA JR Nigeria Flood Response</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">According to the Emergency Operations Centre (EOC), Borno State remains the most severely affected by flooding in Nigeria, with nearly 400,000 IDPs registered in 30 relocation sites as of 18/09. These displacements followed the collapse of the Alau Dam on 09/09, which triggered widespread flooding in the Maiduguri, Jere, and Konduga local government areas. Among those affected are at least 44,000 children &gt; five, including 7,700 suffering from SAM and 18,100 with MAM, as well as 24,000 pregnant &lt;(&gt;&amp;&lt;)&gt; breastfeeding womenand girls who require urgent, life-saving assistance over the next 2 months. A desk review conducted by Oxfam &lt;(&gt;&amp;&lt;)&gt; local partners highlights that the number of people in relocation sites is steadily increasing, with many sites becoming overcrowded, leading to open defecation &lt;(&gt;&amp;&lt;)&gt; raising significant concerns about potential outbreaks of cholera and malaria due to poor sanitary conditions &lt;(&gt;&amp;&lt;)&gt; stagnant water. Critical needs and gaps due to limited local capacity to currently respond were identified by Oxfam &lt;(&gt;&amp;&lt;)&gt; local partners include WASH, food, ES&lt;(&gt;&amp;&lt;)&gt;NFIs, health, nutrition, and protection services to mitigate the risk of GBV and safeguarding of separated and unaccompanied children. Based on gap analysis and coordination with State &lt;(&gt;&amp;&lt;)&gt; UN agencies, other partners on ground, Oxfam &lt;(&gt;&amp;&lt;)&gt; partners (CRUDAN + CATAI) plan to provide MPCA to address most pressing needs, alongside interventions in WASH and NFIs. The rapid market analysis showed the feasibility of cash. The response aims to reach 35000 people in Borno, adhering to Sphere and cluster standards, with a strong focus on protection and gender mainstreaming. Oxfam + partners have longstanding and expertise in WASH, MPCA, and NFI distribution, including with a conflict sensitive lense. We will closely collaborate with State, UN agencies, DRA and other partners on ground for the referral &lt;(&gt;&amp;&lt;)&gt; complementary efforts to maximize the impact of interventions. We will also mobilize of early recovery efforts.</narrative>
  </description>
  <participating-org role="4" type="90">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Christian Rural and Urban Developme</narrative>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Christian Rural and Urban Development Association of Nigeria(CRUDAN) is a Christian inter-denominational, not-for-profit, Non-Governmental Organization. It is a membership organization that has as its members-Churches, Christian organizations and individuals that are engaged or interested in development work in Nigeria. It has the main goal of promoting the growth of the church in Nigeriaby assisting in rural/urban development work that is part of her witness. CRUDAN serves the church all over Nigeria in Wholistic development work. CRUDAN was formed in 1990, following the merger of two Christian organizations that operated independently in the country, namely Christian Rural Advisory Council(CRAC), Founded in 1965 in the Northern part of Nigeria, and Christian Rural Fellowship of Nigeria(CRFN), established in 1953, in the Southern part of Nigeria. Being a membership organization, the members from the general assembly that elects the board. CRUDAN has five(5) zones within the country namely, Central,Northeast, Northwest, Southeast and Southwest zones with a Zonal facilitator and Administrative Assistant for each zone. With this arrangement, CRUDAN is able to effectively cover the whole nation. CRUDAN began operation in 1991 and was officially registered by the Corporate Affairs Commission(CAC) of the Federal Government of Nigeria on 7th December 1992. Registration number 7111.</narrative>
  </participating-org>
